{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["In evolutionary natural selection, there is no 'agent' making a rational choice; in Popper's science, the scientist actively chooses.","Biological survival is binary (live/die); theory corroboration is a matter of degree and severity of tests.","Evolution has no 'goal' of truth, whereas Popperian science explicitly aims at verisimilitude."],"overturn_conditions":["If it is shown that 'best bet' reasoning implicitly assumes the future resembles the past, the analogy to non-inductive choice fails.","If the concept of 'severity' in testing cannot be defined without reference to background assumptions that are themselves inductive."],"shared_structure":["Selection of an option based on past performance metrics without assuming those metrics predict future outcomes.","Distinction between 'reporting history' and 'betting on the future'.","Reliance on a regulative ideal (truth/survival) rather than a probabilistic calculation."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_004"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a gambler might choose the horse with the best past record not because it guarantees a win, but because it is the 'best bet' available, scientists choose the best-corroborated theory as the best current guess.","The logic of 'survival of the fittest' transfers to 'survival of the most tested,' implying robustness but not immortality."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible without induction if framed as a non-epistemic, methodological rule for selecting the most informative 'best bet' for falsification, rather than a prediction of future success; however, this stance costs the agent any rational assurance that the chosen theory will work in practice, reducing scientific action to a bold gamble on truth-likeness (verisimilitude) that cannot be logically guaranteed.","counterconditions":["If verisimilitude comparisons are logically impossible between false theories (as Tichý and Miller argued), then the basis for preferring one surviving theory over another collapses.","If 'acting' on a theory inherently requires assuming the uniformity of nature (that future tests resemble past ones), then the distinction between critical preference and induction is merely semantic.","If the goal of science shifts from 'truth' to 'reliable prediction,' Popper's account fails to justify why the best-corroborated theory is the most reliable predictor."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism distinguishes between 'corroboration' (a logical report of past test severity and survival) and 'inductive inference' (a probabilistic claim about future recurrence). It posits that preferring the best-corroborated theory is a rational decision based on the goal of finding truth (verisimilitude), where the theory with the highest content surviving the severest tests is logically the closest to truth we have, even if we cannot know it will remain so. This transforms the preference from an inductive expectation ('it worked before, so it will work again') to a critical choice ('this is the most rigorous candidate currently available for our aim of truth').","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}